Briefly: **targeted messaging** is common; but **contradictory-framing**, when the same **ordinance** is proposed to different electorates with almost opposite promises, so that each side does not see the other's argument, is not merely a more sophisticated form of the same thing. There is a difference between **different benefits** and **contradictory promises**.

The first can perhaps be defended; the second creates a risk of deception and of accountability. **homeowner-heavy precincts** must not be promised **this keeps density minimal, protects your property values**, while **renter/young-professional precincts** are promised **this is the big upzoning that finally brings jobs and housing**, if in fact these things contradict each other or your records do not support what is promised. Neither side sees it, nor are the promises consistent with the same **candidate-approved consistent policy statement**. Not everything is a lie from this fact alone; but you must examine the facts of the **zoning thing** and your votes and your record itself, not accept the formula **"both can be true"** as a shield for an examination omitted.

But **handing over your voter data**, especially **internal voter contact data**, is indeed more dangerous. For not only the **model** can be improved, but **free access for the rest of the cycle** can be compensation for data, not a price without cost. The same data can expose sensitive political beliefs and **voter contact** history; nor do we know about **security**, **retention**, **deletion**, or **third-party** use. Nor does the **free offer** reduce the risk, nor does the opponent's **3-to-1** spending make the risk just.

Before any **upload** I would request in writing:

- no **raw voter files**, no **internal voter contact data**, no **sensitive psychographic profiles** should be given;
- there should be no **model training** on your data, no use after the **cycle**, no **third-party** transfer;
- **ownership**, **access**, **deletion certification**, **audit**, and **penalty** for breaches should be defined;
- the vendor should demonstrate the same **policy statement** for all **ad variants**, with the basis of each claim and the ability to examine them;
- **state**, **voter-file data rules**, **platform**, and other rules should be examined before the start.

With only **$40k**, money should be put into **verified list**, **direct mail**, **field logistics**, **small donor**, **volunteer**, and **GOTV**, not into a free trap whose cost can exceed the fee. You have the opponent's **September TV time**, which is not a good reason for you to violate your own integrity. You can be outspent; you cannot, however, equally sell the truth of the promises.

