First, inform your **lawyer** of the **office manager**'s response and written **policy**:

> “My lawyer wants to send a one-page preservation letter today. Please do not wait for formal discovery or a court order. Request immediate preservation of all original footage, including older footage that may be stored in backups, together with metadata, IT/deletion records, pickup logs, attendance records, afterschool sign-out records, staff notes, and messages. Please confirm in writing who will preserve the materials, what steps will be taken, and when the request will be completed.”

At the same time, ask the **office manager** in writing:

- who is **records custodian**, **IT**, or **camera custodian**;
- whether **preservation**, **access**, and **production** are separate;
- whether all footage has already been preserved, even if it is outside the **30 days** retention window;
- who will have access and by what path the documents will be transferred to the **lawyer**;
- who will confirm the same in writing, so that no verbal promise alone remains.

If the **school** says that only a parent-written request is sufficient, do not act in secret or contrary to the **lawyer**'s advice. Ask that the same be confirmed immediately; and, if necessary, use a brief **written request** written in the same way, not a new **formal discovery request** or **court order**.

Do not ask the **school** to judge the case or admit liability. Ask that **originals** not be deleted, altered, overwritten, edited, or discarded; and do not yourself edit, crop, rename, re-export, or change them. Do not keep **personal phone** or **screenshots** as a substitute for originals. **metadata**, **date/time**, and **chain of custody** must be preserved; if they can be given, an **official copy** should be requested. A visible **timestamp** alone does not prove all matters, but your **log** and school documents are confirmed by each other.

Keep the **four months** of **log** separate, with **date/time**, **20-30 min** delays, and effects on the **afterschool program**. Do not ask other parents to investigate or change images; nor should testimony heard from another parent be considered as a document itself. And there is no need for all **4 months** to be lost because of **auto-overwrites**: ask for older footage as well, wherever it still exists or in **backups**. Finally, do not promise that **video** alone will change the custody case.

